<p>U.S.-Iran talks remained stalled after Tehran offered to reopen the Strait of Hormuz if the United States lifted its naval blockade and the war ended, while postponing talks on Iran’s nuclear program until a later stage. The proposal was delivered through Pakistan, and President Trump discussed it with national security advisers, but a U.S. official said he was dissatisfied because he wants nuclear issues addressed from the start. The standoff follows the U.S.-Israeli offensive against Iran that began on Feb. 28 and has left the Strait of Hormuz mostly shut despite a ceasefire, disrupting a waterway that carries about one-fifth of global oil and gas trade in peacetime. Iran has said the strait will not fully reopen while the U.S. blockade remains in place; Washington says any settlement must prevent Iran from moving toward a nuclear weapon. President Trump wrote on Truth Social that Iran had told Washington it was in a “State of Collapse” and wanted the Strait of Hormuz opened quickly while it worked through its leadership situation. News accounts said it was unclear how Iran communicated that message, and Tehran had not immediately confirmed it. Iran’s Deputy Defence Minister Reza Talaei-Nik said the United States was no longer in a position to dictate policy to independent countries.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/DyFwd0VuDjaPkuO55dXbmbwiLFTp59SKRrc1erCPfuOApbbXHbXmAOZ-JJohAUJKJkQegAszqaN2pNWXSn5VEBVQP0EtNTpj3J7Q7vbpENC9aO3mgclpvFRM4IQyEZaW14HJL7ntVCgQp1jsgQHtat9osVZG5dRJpyT_vdX_x8KKlECvu4YPRHM6mY2MQ7k83qAMYpY_5bCKf3mDWeKXBWipgTaa3Nxz_4ib9YDtfPdbXOKeBRQQ6ox4P4ZStUjyasOEugkCO6R7mI8VTQ' alt='Vehicles pass a Tehran billboard reading “The Strait of Hormuz remains closed” on April 28, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Rubio: Secretary of State Marco Rubio described Iran’s use of Hormuz as an “economic nuclear weapon” and said Washington would not legitimize a system in which Tehran decides which ships can use an international waterway.</li><li>Leverage: Reports described a debate inside the Trump administration over whether Iran or the United States is better positioned to withstand the economic costs of a prolonged Strait closure.</li></ul><blockquote>We have to ensure that any deal that is made, any agreement that is made, is one that definitively prevents them from sprinting towards a nuclear weapon at any point - Marco Rubio</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Trump: Trump portrayed the blockade of Iranian ports as a pressure campaign squeezing Tehran’s oil revenue and argued that the pressure was pushing Iran toward an off-ramp. (<a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-04-28-0700/middle-east-trump-says-iran-heading-toward-collapse-hopes-tehran-sorts-out-leadership'>AllSides</a>)</li><li>Iranian government: Tehran framed the talks as stalled because Washington was pursuing what Iran called unlawful and unrealistic demands, while saying the ceasefire had not yet produced a permanent settlement. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/us-no-longer-in-position-to-dictate-policy-to-other-nations-says-iran/article70915569.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Qatar: Qatar framed the crisis as a regional de-escalation issue, saying conflicts in the region should be resolved at the negotiating table and warning against widening the confrontation. (<a href='https://tribune.com.pk/story/2605226/trump-not-happy-with-latest-iran-proposal-to-end-the-war-us-official-says'>The Express Tribune</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-iran-us-war-talks-strait-of-hormuz-2/'>Trump says Iran is figuring out its leadership as talks hit an impasse</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/28/nx-s1-5802283/iran-middle-east-updates'>Deadlock over Iran&#x27;s nuclear program and the Strait of Hormuz cripples peace efforts</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-israel-iran-ceasefire-news-live-updates